## 6. Guidance note on marking AI-generated content

Article 50(2) asks for machine-readable marking of synthetic output. This is the part
teams most often miss, because it is not satisfied by a visible label alone.

**What is being asked for.** Marking that is machine-readable, and that survives normal
handling of the content. A visible disclaimer in the interface satisfies 50(1); it does
not satisfy 50(2), because it does not travel with the content once it is copied out.

**Approaches, roughly in order of practicality for a text-generating system:**

1. **Provenance metadata (C2PA / Content Credentials).** The best developed standard for
   images, audio and video. Attaches signed provenance to the asset. Well supported for
   media, weaker for plain text.
2. **Embedded metadata.** For files your system emits, write the generator and generation
   time into standard metadata fields. Cheap to do, easily stripped, but it is a real
   marking rather than a decorative one.
3. **Watermarking of generated text.** Statistical watermarking exists but is not robust
   to paraphrase or editing, and applying it requires control of the decoding step in the
   model server. For most self-hosted deployments this is not currently practical.
4. **Marking at the boundary where content leaves the system.** Where generated text is
   written into documents, tickets, emails or records, mark it there, a field on the
   record saying which model produced it and when. This is the approach most self-hosted
   deployments can actually implement, and Flugschreiber's request identifier gives you a
   key that ties the marked artefact back to the evidence log.

**Exemptions to check.** The obligation is relaxed where the AI system performs an
assistive function for standard editing and does not substantially alter the input data,
and there are carve-outs for content that undergoes human review and editorial
responsibility. Both are narrower than teams tend to assume. Check the current text.

